---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import NameGenerator from '../components/NameGenerator.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Dragon Name Generator — 72 Fantasy Names with Meanings" description="Choose Fire, Ice, Shadow, Storm, Earth, or Sky dragon names. Generate 4, 6, 8, or 12 names with meanings, then copy a batch for your creative project." breadcrumbs={[{name:'Name Generator',href:'/dragon-name-generator/'}]}>
  <h1>Dragon Name Generator</h1>
  <p class="lead">Find a name for your next fictional dragon: a quiet winter companion, a restless storm traveler, or a creature of the open sky. Explore 72 fantasy names across six styles, each with a one-sentence meaning.</p>
  <NameGenerator />
  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Names for novels, games, and D&amp;D</h2>
    <p>For a novel or short story, start with the setting and the sound of the character's name. Embercrest might suggest a warm, bright presence, while Snowhush offers a quieter mood. Say your shortlist aloud alongside the names of your other characters. Distinct opening sounds can help readers tell a large cast apart, especially when several names appear close together.</p>
    <p>For a game character, companion, or username, a compact name such as Sunglide can be easier to type and remember. Try a longer compound for a title or a dramatic introduction. Check the game's naming rules and whether your chosen name is available before settling on it.</p>
    <p>For a D&amp;D campaign, choose a name that suits your group's setting and agree on its pronunciation. The meaning can be a story prompt: Mossridge suggests a woodland landscape, while Rainforge evokes rough weather and movement. Your group decides the character's role, personality, and history; the style label adds atmosphere without setting game rules.</p>
    <h2>Choosing a style and meaning</h2>
    <p>Fire emphasizes warmth and sparks; Ice favors stillness and winter textures. Shadow uses twilight imagery, while Storm draws on thunder and rain. Earth brings together stone and woodland details, and Sky leans toward clouds, sunlight, and open horizons. Mix styles when a character needs an unexpected contrast.</p>
    <p>The meanings are creative interpretations of English fantasy compounds. You can keep a meaning as a private writing cue, adapt it into a nickname, or give the name a different meaning inside your own fictional setting. These suggestions are general creative material and do not identify characters from the Fourth Wing novels.</p>
    <h2>Save your shortlist</h2>
    <p>Choose a style, select 4, 6, 8, or 12, and generate a batch. Each batch contains distinct names from the collection; later batches may include previous favorites. Copy names and meanings together into your notes, then compare the sound, spelling, and mood before making a choice.</p>
    <p>The generator runs in your browser without an account. It does not upload or save your choices. The copy button writes to your clipboard only when you select it; if clipboard access is unavailable, a selectable text box appears instead. Names can overlap with existing uses, so check your final choice before publishing a commercial project.</p>
    <h2>More fan tools</h2>
    <p>Try the <a href="/quiz/dragon-type/">dragon personality quiz</a> for another creative starting point, or browse the <a href="/dragons/">color and tail vocabulary index</a>. The <a href="/fourth-wing-characters/">Fourth Wing character index</a> is a separate reference for publisher-sourced names.</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
